WEBVTT

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:07.830
<v Ira Glass>OK. So a couple of physicists walk into a bar. Just kidding, they're not at the bar. They're at a school. Scientists in training.
<v Ira Glass>好的。话说有几个物理学家走进了一家酒吧。开个玩笑，他们没去酒吧。他们在学校里。那是正在受训的科学家们。

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:17.070
<v David Kestenbaum>I'm sure this story seems strange to you. But, to me, it was just-- you know, this is just like another day [LAUGHS] in the physics world.
<v David Kestenbaum>我相信这个故事对你们来说很奇怪。但对我来说，这只是——你知道，这就像物理学界的日常[笑声]一样。

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:40.920
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum used to live in the physics world. These days, he lives in our world. He's a journalist-producer here at our show. But at the time of this story, he was getting a PhD in high energy particle physics at-- there's no way to avoid the name droppiness of what I'm about to say-- at Harvard University. But to paraphrase Us Magazine, Harvard physicists, in certain ways around the office, they're just like us.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）曾经生活在物理学界。如今，他生活在我们的世界里。他是我们节目的记者兼制片人。但在故事发生时，他正在攻读高能粒子物理学博士学位——我说出这些话时，完全无法掩饰那种想炫耀背景的感觉——就是在哈佛大学。不过套用《美国周刊》的话说，哈佛的物理学家们，在某些办公场合，他们其实也和我们一样。

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:59.880
<v David Kestenbaum>There's always a time of day when someone made a pot of very strong coffee. And afterwards, everyone drank the coffee and then didn't quite want to work yet. And so we all stood around and talked about various things. You know, and there was a blackboard, or a whiteboard, or something there. And we were talking about how nobody really had girlfriends.
<v David Kestenbaum>每天总有一段时间，会有人冲上一大壶浓咖啡。之后，每个人都喝了咖啡，然后还没完全进入工作状态。于是我们大家都站在一起，聊各种各样的事情。你知道，那里有一块黑板，或者白板，之类的东西。我们当时在谈论为什么没人有女朋友。

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:11.430
<v Ira Glass>So, this being physics world, the next logical thing to do was to employ the power of mathematics to estimate the likelihood of finding a girlfriend. And so they start jotting down a calculation.
<v Ira Glass>所以，作为物理学界，接下来的逻辑就是运用数学的力量来估算找到女朋友的概率。于是他们开始在板上草拟计算过程。

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.970
<v David Kestenbaum>I guess it's sort of a variation on-- you know this thing called the Drake equation?
<v David Kestenbaum>我想这算是某种变体——你知道德雷克方程（Drake equation）吗？

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.410
<v Ira Glass>No.
<v Ira Glass>不知道。

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:23.520
<v David Kestenbaum>That is a way to estimate how many planets are out there that have intelligent life on them.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是用来估算外星存在多少智慧生命的一种方法。

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:45.890
<v Ira Glass>OK. So in this Drake equation, apparently you start with how many stars are in the universe. That is, all the places where there might be life, and then you subtract out all the stars that don't have planets around them, right? Because there can't be life there. And then you subtract out all the planets that are too far from the sun, or too close to the sun to support life, and so on, and so on. You get the idea. Until finally you come up with the likelihood of a planet with life evolved to the point of intelligence.
<v Ira Glass>好的。在德雷克方程中，显然你要从宇宙中有多少颗恒星开始算起。也就是所有可能存在生命的地方，然后减去周围没有行星的恒星，对吧？因为那里不可能有生命。然后减去所有离太阳太远，或者离太阳太近而无法维持生命的行星，以此类推，等等。你明白这个意思吧。直到最后，你得出一个行星上演化出智慧生命的概率。

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:56.790
<v Ira Glass>OK. They ran the same kind of math now except-- and I realize this is going to sound a little strange. But he say they replaced intelligent life with girlfriends.
<v Ira Glass>好的。他们现在用了同样的数学方法，只是——我知道这听起来有点奇怪。但他说是把“智慧生命”换成了“女朋友”。

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:03.550
<v David Kestenbaum>So I think we started to do the calculation on the board. And can you look up what the population of Boston is?
<v David Kestenbaum>我想我们开始在黑板上做计算了。你能查一下波士顿的人口是多少吗？

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:22.050
<v Ira Glass>Now Dave is asking me to look this up because at this point in our interview I actually made him run the math for me with real numbers that we got from the internet. So he started with the population of Boston, because he and his fellow physics students wanted girlfriends in Boston, where they all lived. The population of Boston, I found online, was a little under 600,000.
<v Ira Glass>大卫现在让我查这个，因为在采访的这一刻，我实际上让他用我们从网上找到的真实数字为我演示了计算过程。所以他从波士顿的人口开始，因为他和他的物理学同学想要在波士顿这个他们居住的地方找女朋友。我上网查到，波士顿的人口略低于60万。

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:27.780
<v David Kestenbaum>So you start with 600,000, which sounds great, except that half of them are guys, right? And I'm only interested in girls.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以你从60万开始，这听起来不错，但其中一半是男性，对吧？而我只对女性感兴趣。

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.490
<v Ira Glass>OK. So it's 300,000.
<v Ira Glass>好的。所以是30万。

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:35.550
<v David Kestenbaum>And then I want people-- let's be honest, probably within 10 years of my age or something, right?
<v David Kestenbaum>然后我想要的人——老实说，大概在和我年龄相差10岁以内的吧，对吧？

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.250
<v Ira Glass>OK. So 10 years on either side. So that means--
<v Ira Glass>好的。也就是上下10岁。那意味着——

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:47.350
<v David Kestenbaum>I'm actually looking at some numbers here. It looks like if you go from 20 to 40, you're talking-- that's still like 35% of the population, a third or something.
<v David Kestenbaum>我正在看一些数据。看起来如果你选20到40岁之间，你谈论的——那大约仍占总人口的35%，也就是三分之一左右。

00:02:47.350 --> 00:03:04.920
<v Ira Glass>So that means that out of 300,000 women, that leaves 100,000 in his age range. These being doctoral students they wanted girlfriends who were college grads. Well, OK. About 25% of Americans over 25 years old have graduated from college. That knocks out roughly three-fourths of these women.
<v Ira Glass>所以这意味着在30万女性中，他这个年龄段的还有10万人。作为博士生，他们想要大学毕业的女朋友。嗯，好吧。25岁以上的美国人中约有25%大学毕业。这排除了大约四分之三的女性。

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:05.670
<v David Kestenbaum>Ouch.
<v David Kestenbaum>哎哟。

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.870
<v Ira Glass>So you're down to-- we were at 100,000. So you're down to 25,000.
<v Ira Glass>所以你只剩下——我们之前是10万。所以现在只剩下2.5万了。

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:15.690
<v David Kestenbaum>Then you start applying stuff like, you know, how often are they single?
<v David Kestenbaum>然后你开始应用一些条件，比如，你知道，她们单身的频率是多少？

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.730
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Let's say half of them are single. So now you're down to 12,500.
<v Ira Glass>对。假设她们中有一半是单身。所以现在只剩下12500人了。

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.160
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, see? It's getting scary now, right?
<v David Kestenbaum>是吧，看？现在开始变得可怕了，对吧？

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:36.030
<v Ira Glass>And then, of course, you get to how many people are actually attractive to you. And even if you give a really high percentage-- like one in five, OK? That knocks your pool of candidates down from 12,500 to 2,500.
<v Ira Glass>然后，当然，还要算上实际上对你有吸引力的人数。即使你给出一个很高的比例——比如五分之一，好吗？这会让你的候选池从12500缩减到2500人。

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.130
<v David Kestenbaum>In the whole city of Boston, right?
<v David Kestenbaum>在整个波士顿市，对吧？

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.010
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.200
<v David Kestenbaum>That's just like a needle in a haystack.
<v David Kestenbaum>这简直就像大海捞针。

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:55.680
<v Ira Glass>And that 2,500 is before you get to anything personal, like your religion, or how you see the world, what's your sense of humor. So Dave and his fellow students are talking about this-- these rather kind of, you know, depressing numbers. And one of the professors comes in.
<v Ira Glass>而这2500人还没算上任何个人因素，比如你的宗教信仰、你的世界观、你的幽默感。所以大卫和他的同学们正在讨论这些——这些相当令人沮丧的数字。这时一位教授进来了。

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:16.460
<v David Kestenbaum>She's not married either. And so we start to draw it for her. And then we started to say, well, OK, half of them are men. So we'd circle half. And then we'd say, well, what's the age group you're interested in? And then we'd sort of circle a smaller subset. And then she had all these other requirements, like the guy had to be taller than her-- and she's pretty tall-- so that really limited things.
<v David Kestenbaum>她也没有结婚。于是我们开始为她画这个计算图。然后我们开始说，好吧，一半是男性。所以我们画了一个圆圈代表一半。然后我们说，那么你感兴趣的年龄段是多少？然后我们画了一个更小的子集。接着她还有其他所有要求，比如那个男人必须比她高——她挺高的——所以这真的限制了很多选择。

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:24.920
<v David Kestenbaum>And then she said he had to be smarter than her. You know, and she's a Harvard physics professor. So that was even smaller. And basically we got down to there being nobody. [LAUGHS]
<v David Kestenbaum>然后她说他必须比她聪明。你知道，她可是哈佛的物理学教授。所以范围就更小了。基本上我们最后得出结论，根本没人选。[笑声]

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:28.640
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:34.730
<v David Kestenbaum>She's alone.
<v David Kestenbaum>她独自一人。

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:42.540
<v Ira Glass>During this period of your life when you would think about these numbers, were you sure the entire time that there was somebody out there?
<v Ira Glass>在你人生中考虑这些数字的这段时间里，你是否一直确信这世上会有那么一个人存在？

00:04:42.540 --> 00:05:06.320
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah. I don't know why. But, you know, at the beginning of every mathematical proof, people often write, assume that there exists X. [LAUGHS] Assume we have an infinite surface bound by something or whatever. It's like, assume there exists some girlfriend. There is totally that act of faith underneath it. Yeah.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。我不知道为什么。但是，你知道，在每个数学证明的开头，人们经常写“假设存在X”。[笑声] 假设我们有一个被某物包围的无限表面之类的。这就像是，假设存在某个女朋友。这完全是一种信仰行为。是的。

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:15.960
<v David Kestenbaum>But I had a more scientific view, which is that there are people out there, you know, who might be right for me. And not just one person. That seemed like some-- in a silly novel or something. You know?
<v David Kestenbaum>但我有一个更科学的观点，那就是外面确实有人，你知道，可能适合我。而且不仅仅是一个人。那听起来像是——在愚蠢的小说里才会发生。你知道吗？

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:16.950
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:31.780
<v David Kestenbaum>I don't believe there's just one-- if there were just one person out there, good luck! They could speak Chinese, you know? And they probably do, right? What are the odds you're going to find them and the translator? You've got to believe there's more than one person.
<v David Kestenbaum>我不相信世界上只有一个——如果世界上真的只有一个人，祝你好运！他们可能会说中文，你知道吗？而且他们很可能确实说了，对吧？你找到他们和翻译的几率有多大？你必须相信不止一个人。

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:49.420
<v Ira Glass>Well, today in our program we have stories of people who dare to believe that they have found that one person for them, even if it is just a pleasant lie, that there is just one person out there for each of us. They have chosen to believe that they need to look no further. And then they act on that belief, doing some things, I have to say, I bet you have never heard of people doing.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的节目中，我们有关于那些敢于相信自己已经找到了那唯一一个人的故事，即使这只是一个美好的谎言，即我们每个人在世上都只有那么一个命中注定的人。他们选择相信自己无需再寻找。然后他们根据这种信念采取行动，做一些我敢说你从未听说过的事情。

00:05:49.420 --> 00:06:06.790
<v Ira Glass>And they do these things because they just cannot stop themselves. Like every country song ever written would tell you, they have got no choice. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. We first broadcast today's episode in 2013. We're running it again today because Valentine's Day is coming around again.
<v Ira Glass>他们做这些是因为他们无法控制自己。就像所有乡村音乐所写的那样，他们别无选择。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。我们于2013年首次播出本期节目。我们今天再次重播，因为情人节又要到了。

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:32.220
<v Ira Glass>The population of Boston has grown since 2013 when we first did this show. It's now almost 700,000. Meaning the odds of finding love there have gotten even worse, if you believe there's just one person for you. So what if that's true? Answers today. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>自2013年我们首次制作这期节目以来，波士顿的人口有所增长。现在几乎达到70万了。这意味着如果你相信世界上只有一个人属于你，那么在那里找到真爱的几率变得更低了。如果那是真的怎么办？今天的节目将为您解答。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:45.280
<v Ira Glass>Act One, The Best Laid Plans. Well, when you hear it the first couple did-- the thing that they decided to attempt-- I think one of the things that is the most incredible about it is the kind of couple they were when they did this, which is a really great couple.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，精心策划。嗯，当你听到这对情侣做了什么——他们决定尝试的事情——我认为最令人不可思议的一点是，当他们这样做时，他们是一对什么样的情侣，也就是一对真的很棒的情侣。

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.870
<v Kurt Braunohler>We were always like the most solid of all the relationships of our friends and everything like that.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我们总是我们所有朋友中关系最稳固的，诸如此类。

00:06:49.870 --> 00:07:06.310
<v Ira Glass>Kurt Braunohler met his girlfriend on the third day of college. And, in all of the ways that we think of what makes a good relationship, I think that they were doing better than you, or me, or most people. They had a lot of fun together. They could talk about anything. They didn't really fight. Happy sex life.
<v Ira Glass>库尔特·布劳诺勒（Kurt Braunohler）在大学第三天就遇到了他的女朋友。而且，在所有我们认为构成良好关系的方面，我认为他们做得比你、我或者大多数人都好。他们在一起很快乐。他们无话不谈。他们几乎不吵架。性生活也很美满。

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:23.860
<v Kurt Braunohler>We really just got along very well together. We traveled well together. You know, we were always the kind of-- we were the place that friends would come when they are having hard times and they would stay with us. And we were kind of-- it felt like very adult. Even when I was 23, I felt like, I'm kind of married, even though neither of us had ever, ever discussed getting married.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我们真的相处得非常好。我们一起去旅行。你知道，我们一直是那种——当朋友们遇到困难时，他们会来我们这里，会和我们待在一起。而且我们感觉——感觉非常成熟。即使在我23岁时，我就觉得，我像是结婚了，尽管我们俩从未讨论过结婚的事。

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.810
<v Ira Glass>So what happened?
<v Ira Glass>那后来发生了什么？

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:48.260
<v Kurt Braunohler>Well, after we had both turned 30, one day I just kind of was thinking about why we had never talked about getting married. Like, we had never, ever talked about it seriously or otherwise. And whenever anyone would ask us, we would always just kind of brush it off and say, oh, well, we're going to-- we'll get married when we have kids. That's what we'd always say.
<v Kurt Braunohler>嗯，在我们俩都过了30岁以后，有一天我突然在想为什么我们从来没有谈过结婚的事。就像，我们从来没有认真地或者其他方式谈论过它。每当有人问起我们，我们总是敷衍过去，说，哦，好吧，我们打算——我们要等有了孩子再结婚。这就是我们一直说的。

00:07:48.260 --> 00:08:13.480
<v Kurt Braunohler>And I remember we were sitting in the living room and it was October. And I just said, hey, I want to talk. And I said, why-- you know, why do you think we haven't gotten married yet? Or even talked about it? And she just kind of looked at me and thought for a second. And then she said, well, I think that before we get married, we should probably sleep with other people.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我还记得我们坐在客厅里，那是十月。我只是说，嘿，我想谈谈。我说，为什么——你知道，你觉得我们为什么还没结婚呢？甚至都没谈过？她看着我，想了一会儿。然后她说，嗯，我觉得在结婚之前，我们大概应该和其他人睡一觉。

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:29.970
<v Kurt Braunohler>And-- and that-- you would imagine that that would come back as a huge shock to me, like hit me hard. But for some reason, it kind of-- it made sense to me. I kind of was like, OK. Very calmly. And I guess the reason--
<v Kurt Braunohler>然后——然后那——你会认为这会对我造成巨大的冲击，就像猛击我一样。但由于某种原因，它——对我来说说得通。我当时就像，好吧。非常冷静地。我想原因——

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.390
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. How did that make sense?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。这怎么就说得通了？

00:08:33.390 --> 00:09:02.950
<v Kurt Braunohler>I've thought a lot about this. And I think that there's a few reasons. We both had this kind of arrogant notion of our relationship-- that it could survive literally anything. That we had been together-- we had known each other our whole adult lives. We were each other's worlds. So really I don't think we thought that we could destroy this thing.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我对此思考了很多。我认为有几个原因。我们俩对我们的关系有一种傲慢的观念——认为它几乎能经受任何考验。我们在一起——我们互相了解了整个成年生活。我们是彼此的世界。所以我真的认为我们从没想过我们会毁掉这段关系。

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:26.500
<v Ira Glass>I talked to the girlfriend, who did not want to come on the radio. And she said basically she'd only had two boyfriends in her life-- her high school boyfriend, who was not a good boyfriend at all, and Kurt, who was great. But she met Kurt, she said, when she was 17. She was 30 when this happened. And she said that she felt like maybe she missed out on something in her 20s, experiences that other people had. And she didn't want to regret that.
<v Ira Glass>我和那位女朋友谈过，她不想上电台。她说基本上她一生中只有过两个男朋友——她的高中男友，那根本不是个好男友，还有库尔特，他很棒。但她说她在17岁时遇到了库尔特。这件事发生时她30岁。她说她觉得也许她在20多岁时错过了什么，错过了别人拥有的那些经历。她不想为此后悔。

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:37.630
<v Ira Glass>A warning, I should say, before we go any further in this story. We're going to acknowledge the existence of sex between adults. Nothing explicit. Anyway, so they sat on the couch, and they talked this through.
<v Ira Glass>在继续讲这个故事之前，我应该先提个醒。我们将承认成年人之间性行为的存在。没有什么露骨的细节。总之，他们坐在沙发上，商量了这件事。

00:09:37.630 --> 00:10:09.150
<v Kurt Braunohler>The next part of that conversation was how-- the logistics. Do we break up? Do I move out? Do we just do this while we're living in the same house together? And we kind of came up with this idea of borrowing this Amish concept called rumspringa. And rumspringa in the Amish world is when you're 16, you're allowed to be not Amish for two years. And then when you turn 18, you decide whether or not you come back to the fold.
<v Kurt Braunohler>谈话的下一部分是如何——后勤工作。我们分手吗？我要搬出去吗？我们要住在同一栋房子里时就这样做吗？我们想出了借用阿米什人“青春期漫游”（rumspringa）概念的想法。在阿米什世界里，青春期漫游是指当你16岁时，你可以有两年不当阿米什人。然后当你18岁时，你再决定是否回归。

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:14.130
<v Ira Glass>Right. And during those two years, kids get drunk. They sleep around. They try drugs.
<v Ira Glass>对。在那两年里，孩子们会喝醉。他们会滥交。他们会尝试毒品。

