<v Kurt Braunohler>And we woke up in the morning and we went and got brunch. And we brought it to the park, the way we'd always used to do on Saturdays. And we just sat in the park and talked. And we broke up. And that's the thing. I can't I can't point to something that was bad about our relationship, except for knowing that I feel like it had run its course.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我们早上醒来去吃了早午餐。我们把它带到公园，就像我们以前周六习惯做的那样。我们只是坐在公园里交谈。然后我们分手了。这就是事情的经过。我不能——我不能指出我们关系中有什么不好的地方，除了知道我感觉它已经走到了尽头。

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:20.160
<v Kurt Braunohler>I mean, we organically got to the point where we both thought this was a good idea.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我是说，我们自然而然地达到了我们俩都认为这是个好主意的地步。

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:22.170
<v Ira Glass>This-- the rumspringa?
<v Ira Glass>这个——那个青春期漫游吗？

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:41.610
<v Kurt Braunohler>It's almost like we-- the relationship had an expiration date, but we didn't know about it. And then all of a sudden, we came up with this really complicated, crazy plan to put the test down at the right place where it would break. And it broke.
<v Kurt Braunohler>这简直就像我们——这段关系有一个保质期，但我们不知道。然后突然之间，我们想出了一个非常复杂、疯狂的计划，把测试放在了它会破裂的地方。然后它就破裂了。

00:22:41.610 --> 00:23:05.550
<v Ira Glass>His old girlfriend said something similar. She said she'd probably wanted out of the relationship before the rumspringa, but she was just too scared to admit it to herself. The rumspringa gave them a way to break up. Kurt says he would not want to do a rumspringa again. But he came out of this experience believing that it is important to force things to a decision with someone, and that it's healthy for any relationship to be evaluated now and then.
<v Ira Glass>他的前女友也说了类似的话。她说她可能在青春期漫游之前就想结束这段关系，但她太害怕承认了。青春期漫游给他们提供了一种分手的方式。库尔特说他不想再进行一次青春期漫游了。但他从这次经历中得出结论，重要的是要强迫自己与某人做出决定，而且偶尔对每一段关系进行评估是健康的。

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:27.750
<v Kurt Braunohler>I do have a theory now. I do have a theory about if I do get married in the future. What I think I would want to do is have an agreement that, at the end of seven years, we have to get remarried in order for the marriage to continue. But at the end of seven years, it ends. And we can agree to get remarried or not get remarried.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我现在确实有一个理论。如果我将来结婚，我有一个理论。我想做的是达成一个协议，即在七年后，我们必须重新结婚，婚姻才能继续。但七年后，它就结束了。我们可以同意重新结婚，或者不同意。

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:29.070
<v Ira Glass>Why?
<v Ira Glass>为什么？

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:36.250
<v Kurt Braunohler>Because then I get-- I think you get to choose. And I think it makes the relationship-- would make the relationship stronger.
<v Kurt Braunohler>因为那样我就能——我认为你拥有了选择的权利。我认为这会让关系——会让关系变得更强。

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:58.990
<v Ira Glass>I don't know what I think of that because I think, actually, one of the things that's a comfort in marriage is that there isn't a door at seven years. And so if something is messed up in the short term, there's a comfort of knowing, like, well, we made this commitment. And so we're just going to work this out. Like, if even if tonight we're not getting along or there's something between us that doesn't feel right, you have the comfort of knowing, like, we've got time.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道我怎么看这个，因为我认为，实际上，婚姻的一种安慰在于七年后没有一道门。所以如果短期内出了问题，有一种安慰感，比如，嗯，我们做了这个承诺。所以我们只是要解决它。就像，即使今晚我们相处得不好，或者我们之间有什么感觉不对劲的地方，你有一种安慰感，知道，嗯，我们有时间。

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:06.730
<v Ira Glass>We're going to figure this out. And that makes it so much easier. Because you do go through, like, times when you hate each other's guts. You know what I mean? Like--
<v Ira Glass>我们会解决这个问题的。这让一切变得容易多了。因为你确实会经历比如互相厌恶的时候。你知道我的意思吗？就像——

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:07.750
<v Kurt Braunohler>Of course you do. Yeah.
<v Kurt Braunohler>你当然会。对。

00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:16.360
<v Ira Glass>And the no escape clause, weirdly, is a bigger comfort to being married than I ever would have thought, before I got married.
<v Ira Glass>而没有逃跑条款，奇怪的是，这对婚姻的安慰比我结婚前想象的要大得多。

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:17.060
<v Kurt Braunohler>Really?
<v Kurt Braunohler>真的吗？

00:24:17.060 --> 00:24:17.470
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:20.430
<v Kurt Braunohler>I never thought of it that way.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我从来没那样想过。

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:21.380
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. It's--
<v Ira Glass>对。它是——

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:37.430
<v Kurt Braunohler>I like thinking about it that way. You just see so many examples of where that-- of people-- where people don't think that way.
<v Kurt Braunohler>我喜欢那样思考。你看到太多例子，人们——人们不是那样思考的。

00:24:37.430 --> 00:25:03.780
<v Ira Glass>Both Kurt and his girlfriend feel like the rumspringa got them where they needed to go as a couple, even if that place was different than they intended when they began the whole thing. Sometimes what you need to do next as a couple you can't even tell what it should be. And the best you can do is hold your breath, make a guess, and jump.
<v Ira Glass>库尔特和他的女朋友都觉得青春期漫游作为一对情侣让他们达到了他们需要去的地方，即使那个地方与他们刚开始整个事情时所预想的不同。有时你作为一对情侣接下来需要做什么，你甚至无法分辨应该是什么。而你能做的最好的事情就是屏住呼吸，猜一猜，然后跳下去。

