00:25:56.960 --> 00:26:10.170
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, for Valentine's Day, stories about people who believe in love, who believe they have found someone for them, and who take action. We have arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, 21 Chump Street.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天在我们的节目中，为了情人节，故事讲述了那些相信爱情、相信自己找到了命中注定的人，并采取行动的人。我们现在进入了节目的第二幕。第二幕，21条笨蛋街（21 Chump Street）。

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:31.510
<v Ira Glass>A couple of years ago, at three high schools in Palm Beach County, Florida, a bunch of young police officers were sent undercover to pose as students. These were recent graduates of the police cadet program, and they blended right in. They went to classes, they ate in the cafeteria. They hung out on the basketball court during recess. They had fake Facebook accounts, of course. They even took the state's standardized test, the FCAT.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，在佛罗里达州棕榈滩县的三所高中，一群年轻的警官被派往学校卧底，冒充学生。他们是警察学员项目的应届毕业生，看起来完全融入了学校。他们去上课，在食堂吃饭。他们在课间休息时在篮球场晃悠。他们当然有虚假的Facebook账号。他们甚至参加了州标准化的FCAT考试。

00:26:31.510 --> 00:26:51.850
<v Ira Glass>Police officers were there undercover because parents and principals had complained about drugs being sold at the schools. Only the principals at the schools knew of their presence. No teachers knew it. No parents knew it. Now, maybe it is hard to imagine a love story coming out of this situation, but one did. A love story where somebody went out of his way, and took action at a key moment for love.
<v Ira Glass>警察之所以去卧底，是因为家长和校长投诉学校有人在卖毒品。只有学校的校长知道他们的存在。没有老师知道。没有家长知道。现在，也许很难想象这种情况会产生爱情故事，但确实发生了。一个某人为了爱情在关键时刻不惜一切代价、采取行动的爱情故事。

00:26:51.850 --> 00:27:02.290
<v Ira Glass>We've told this story twice on our program. Once we turned it into a musical, once as straight reporting. That's what you're about to hear-- the straight reporting version. Robbie Brown was a reporter with the New York Times when he first looked into this.
<v Ira Glass>我们在节目中讲过两次这个故事。一次我们把它改编成了音乐剧，一次是直接报道。这就是你即将听到的——直接报道版本。罗比·布朗（Robbie Brown）是《纽约时报》的记者，他首次调查了这件事。

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:21.580
<v Robbie Brown>The plan was called Operation D-Minus. And one of the schools included in the plan was Park Vista Community High School, where a kid named Justin LaBoy, an 18-year-old honor roll student, was in the last semester of his senior year. And Justin could hardly believe his luck when a very pretty new girl-- everyone had noticed her-- showed up in not just one, but two of his classes.
<v Robbie Brown>该计划被称为“D-Minus行动”。该计划包括的学校之一是公园景观社区高中（Park Vista Community High School），一个名叫贾斯汀·拉博伊（Justin LaBoy）的孩子，一个18岁的优等生，当时正处于高三的最后一个学期。当一个非常漂亮的新女孩——大家都注意到了她——出现在他的不止一门，而是两门课上时，贾斯汀简直不敢相信自己的运气。

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:40.730
<v Justin Laboy>The name that she used was Naomi. I think it was Naomi Rodriguez. And she sat in front of me, but then I sat next to her. So I started talking to her. Oh, hey. My name is Justin, you know? Oh, I'm Naomi. I'm from New York. This and that. Oh, really? Me too. And I had a hat that said New York on it, too, because I was trying to show her that I wasn't lying or faking it.
<v Justin Laboy>她用的名字是娜奥米。我想是娜奥米·罗德里格斯。她坐在我前面，但后来我坐在了她旁边。所以我开始和她交谈。哦，嘿。我叫贾斯汀，你知道吗？哦，我是娜奥米。我来自纽约。这那的。哦，真的吗？我也是。我当时也戴着一顶写着“纽约”的帽子，因为我想向她证明我没有在撒谎或装腔作势。

00:27:40.730 --> 00:28:04.630
<v Justin Laboy>She said she was from Queens. I'm from the Bronx. And she was Puerto Rican and Dominican. And that's what caught me even more. I was like, oh, man. OK. And then I was Puerto Rican, and I was talking to her a little Spanish. And she talked to me a little Spanish. And then we just-- I just got really close to her. I got attached real quick. I was like, wow, look at this, you know?
<v Justin Laboy>她说她来自皇后区。我来自布朗克斯。而且她是波多黎各人和多米尼加人。这更吸引我了。我当时想，噢，伙计。好吧。然后我是波多黎各人，我和她说了点西班牙语。她也跟我说了点西班牙语。然后我们就——我就和她非常亲近了。我很快就粘上了她。我就想，哇，看看这个，你知道吗？

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:06.820
<v Robbie Brown>What did she look like?
<v Robbie Brown>她长什么样？

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:42.790
<v Justin Laboy>She was about 5'4, black straight hair, light skinned. She was mature in the body-wise. So I mean, that caught a lot of people's attention. But the things that she did that I thought that made her a real student was that she would sleep in class. She wouldn't do her homework. I would have to wake her up all the time. Be like, hey, listen. Wake up. You're going to get in trouble. Hey, oh, you didn't do your homework? OK, I'll let you copy my homework. OK.
<v Justin Laboy>她大约5英尺4英寸，黑色直发，皮肤白皙。她在身体上很成熟。所以，我是说，这吸引了很多人的注意力。但我认为她作为一名真正的学生所做的事情是，她会在课堂上睡觉。她不做作业。我得一直叫醒她。就像，嘿，听着。醒醒。你会惹麻烦的。嘿，哦，你没做作业？好的，我让你抄我的作业。好的。

00:28:42.790 --> 00:29:02.200
<v Justin Laboy>Because I mean, I take my classes, you know, seriously, you know? But with her? It was different. I was just like, oh, man. You know, wow, you know. She's cute. I could talk to her, you know? I'll get in trouble once in a while. They'd be like, Justin, stop talking. I'd be like, OK.
<v Justin Laboy>因为我是说，我认真对待我的课程，你知道吗？但对她？那不一样。我只是想，哦，伙计。你知道，哇，你知道。她很可爱。我可以和她说话，你知道吗？我会偶尔惹麻烦。他们会说，贾斯汀，别说话。我会说，好的。

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:17.830
<v Robbie Brown>Justin's a good looking guy, popular and well-liked. He'd had girlfriends before, but nothing that lasted. Nothing that seemed worth holding on to. But Naomi seemed different. She was a good listener. She didn't seem to gossip or play games. She seemed very adult.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀是个长相帅气的家伙，受欢迎且人缘好。他以前交过女朋友，但没有一段能长久。没什么值得留恋的。但娜奥米似乎不同。她是个很好的倾听者。她似乎不爱说长道短或玩弄手段。她看起来非常成熟。

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:40.730
<v Justin Laboy>It's the maturity level that I really cared for. And-- OK, I told her a lot of my feelings. You know, I told her how I felt for her. I mean, I'm not the type of person to open up to you unless we have a really good relationship. And [LAUGHS] I was just opening up so much. And I told her about all my whole life story, about my parents. I told the problems I had.
<v Justin Laboy>这是我真正关心的成熟度。而且——好吧，我告诉了她很多我的感受。你知道，我告诉了她我对她的感觉。我是说，除非我们有非常好的关系，否则我不是那种会向你敞开心扉的人。而且[笑声]我只是敞开了那么多。我告诉了她我所有的生活故事，关于我的父母。我告诉了她我遇到的问题。

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:47.150
<v Robbie Brown>What was it about her that made you trust her? That made you feel like you could open up to her?
<v Robbie Brown>她有什么特质让你信任她？让你觉得你可以向她敞开心扉？

00:29:47.150 --> 00:30:18.290
<v Justin Laboy>She had told me that she lived-- she came up here with her mother, that they was having problems in New York. So you know, they came over here to Florida to settle down, to have a better life. And little stuff like that, to telling people, I believe that, oh, wow. You know, OK, since you're telling me stuff like this, then I could tell you about my life. Yeah. You know, I'm having hard troubles with this person right here. Or I'm having trouble with my parents, or stuff-- little stuff like that makes people bond together, you know?
<v Justin Laboy>她告诉我她住——她和她母亲来到这里，她们在纽约遇到了问题。所以你知道，她们来到佛罗里达是为了安顿下来，过上更好的生活。像这样的小事，告诉人们，我相信，哦，哇。你知道，好的，既然你告诉了我这样的事，那么我也可以告诉你关于我的生活。是的。你知道，我在这儿正和一个某人遇到大麻烦。或者我和父母之间有麻烦，或者诸如此类的小事让人们结成纽带，你知道吗？

00:30:18.290 --> 00:30:31.510
<v Robbie Brown>Justin believed he was seeing signs that Naomi was starting to like him-- like him, like him. They walked together in the halls. They would text during class. He says she even let him kiss her on the cheek. And he serenaded her in front of their entire class.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀相信他看到了娜奥米开始喜欢他的迹象——喜欢他，喜欢他。他们在走廊里一起走。他们会在课上发短信。他说她甚至让他亲了她的脸颊。他还当着全班的面为她唱了小夜曲。

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:46.310
<v Justin Laboy>I'd be like, oh, I'm going to sing you a song. She'd be like, oh, OK. And then I'll just start singing. And then I'll get all blushed up. I'll be like, ah, man, what am I doing? OK. Let me continue. I'll start rapping to her, and I'll start singing. And I mean, I even danced, too. So I danced for her, and I did--
<v Justin Laboy>我会说，哦，我要为你唱首歌。她会说，哦，好的。然后我就开始唱。然后我变得满脸通红。我会想，啊，伙计，我在做什么？好的。让我继续。我会开始给她说唱，我会开始唱歌。我是说，我甚至还跳舞。所以我为她跳舞，我做了——

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:47.510
<v Robbie Brown>You really liked this girl.
<v Robbie Brown>你真的很喜欢这个女孩。

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:54.850
<v Justin Laboy>Man, it tore me apart. I was like, oh my god.
<v Justin Laboy>伙计，它把我撕裂了。我当时想，我的天哪。

