<v Robbie Brown>这一切必然发展到贾斯汀邀请娜奥米参加舞会。

00:31:51.040 --> 00:32:11.440
<v Justin Laboy>Yeah. You know, she's a senior. I'm a senior. Our last year. I don't know where we're going to be after this. So let me just step up, and be a man, and do what I got do. So I went up to her in class and I was like, listen, you know, I know you don't know anybody in this school that much. And I don't want to go with anybody in the school. And you know, I feel really attracted to you. Would you like to come with me to prom?
<v Justin Laboy>是的。你知道，她是高三。我是高三。我们的最后一年。我不知道在这之后我们会去哪里。所以让我站出来，做一个男人，做我该做的事。所以我上课时走到她身边，我说，听着，你知道，我知道你在这个学校不太认识人。而且我不想和学校里的任何人一起去。而且你知道，我对你真的很着迷。你想和我一起去参加舞会吗？

00:32:11.440 --> 00:32:36.850
<v Justin Laboy>Oh, that sounds nice. OK. Excuse me, let me think about it. I was blushing red. I was like, oh, my god. What if she says no? What if she says yes? And after she gave me the OK, yeah, you know, around the more of a yes side, I was-- OK. I was good. Good.
<v Justin Laboy>哦，那听起来不错。好的。打扰一下，让我考虑一下。我脸红了。我心想，噢，我的天哪。如果她说不怎么办？如果她说好怎么办？在她给了我肯定的回答后，是的，你知道，大概算是肯定的那一边，我——好的。我很好。很好。

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:43.340
<v Naomi>You know, prom was coming up and-- I mean, I guess he needed a date. [LAUGHS]
<v Naomi>你知道，舞会快到了，而且——我想他大概需要一个舞伴。[笑声]

00:32:43.340 --> 00:33:07.700
<v Robbie Brown>I interviewed the undercover officer who played Naomi. She's a 25-year-old new recruit with the Palm Beach school's police force. She wouldn't give me her real name, and only agreed to a phone interview, if her supervising sergeant could listen in. She wouldn't talk to me about any specific cases or kids at the school, including Justin. But she did confirm that she was asked to prom. She doesn't remember it quite the way Justin does, though. Mainly, she says she didn't say yes.
<v Robbie Brown>我采访了那个扮演娜奥米的卧底警官。她是棕榈滩学校警察局的一名25岁的新兵。她不愿给我她的真实姓名，并且只有在她的监制警长在场监听的情况下才同意电话采访。她拒绝跟我谈论任何具体的案件或学校里的孩子，包括贾斯汀。但她确实证实了她被邀请参加舞会。不过她对那件事的记忆和贾斯汀的不太一样。主要是，她说她没答应。

00:33:07.700 --> 00:33:30.380
<v Naomi>I told him I wasn't going, just because-- since this is not where I spent the last three years of high school, I didn't really want to go to the prom. And I had to pay for the ticket and everything else, so I was like, nah, it's too much money for me. And he bought it. [LAUGHS]
<v Naomi>我告诉他我不去，只是因为——既然我没有在这里度过高中的最后三年，我真的不想去参加舞会。而且我得付门票钱和其他一切费用，所以我当时想，不，对我来说太多钱了。然后他就信了。[笑声]

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:43.040
<v Robbie Brown>Justin and Naomi didn't end up going to prom, of course, partly because of what happened next. One day at the beginning of April, after Naomi had been at school for about three months, she and Justin had a conversation about drugs.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀和娜奥米当然没能去成舞会，部分原因是接下来发生的事情。四月初的一天，在娜奥米在学校待了大约三个月后，她和贾斯汀聊到了毒品。

00:33:43.040 --> 00:34:00.890
<v Justin Laboy>She asked me if I smoked. And I told her, no, I do not smoke. But if you need anything, I'll be more than happy to help you out. So you know, like I said, I'm not a drug dealer. I don't-- it's hard for me to get drugs at all. So it's not like she told me this day, and then I got it for her the very next day. You know, it took me a while, you know?
<v Justin Laboy>她问我抽不抽烟。我告诉她，不，我不抽烟。但如果你需要什么，我很乐意帮忙。所以你知道，正如我所说，我不是毒贩。我不——我很难搞到毒品。所以并不是她说的那天，我就在第二天立刻就帮她搞到了。你知道，花了我一段时间，你知道？

00:34:00.890 --> 00:34:20.870
<v Justin Laboy>She would text me, and I would text her. She would be like, do you have it yet? No, I don't have it at the moment right now. And she'll be at school, hey, do you have that yet? No, I don't have it right now. I'm sorry. You know, I'm sorry. I'm sorry. I'm really trying like my hardest to get it. And I can't get it, you know, because I like the girl, so--
<v Justin Laboy>她会给我发短信，我也会给她发短信。她会问，你搞到了吗？不，我目前还没有。而她在学校时，嘿，你搞到了吗？不，我现在还没有。我很抱歉。你知道，我很抱歉。我很抱歉。我真的在尽我最大的努力去搞到。但我搞不到，你知道，因为我喜欢这个女孩，所以——

00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:24.980
<v Robbie Brown>What are you thinking as you're trying to get this pot to sell to her?
<v Robbie Brown>你在试图搞到这些大麻去卖给她时，你在想什么？

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:48.790
<v Justin Laboy>I'm thinking, what the heck am I doing? [LAUGHS] Like I never-- I never done this in this school before. So I'm really scared and skeptical about it, too, at the same point. And I'm like, oh, man. OK. OK. So I'm asking my homeboy. And my friend's like, no, I don't know-- I don't know because I don't hang out with guys like that.
<v Justin Laboy>我在想，我到底在干什么？[笑声] 我以前从没在学校里干过这种事。所以我当时也很害怕和怀疑。我就想，噢，伙计。好的。好的。所以我问了我的好哥们。我朋友说，不，我不知道——我不知道，因为我不和那种家伙混在一起。

00:34:48.790 --> 00:35:08.730
<v Robbie Brown>Maybe Justin didn't know guys like that and maybe he did. The police say he told Naomi he smoked pot. Justin says he was just trying to seem cool to impress her. But what's certain is he did find somebody who would sell to him-- a person his cousin knew, he says. So a couple days after they first talked about it, Justin brought a rolled up baggie of marijuana to school for Naomi.
<v Robbie Brown>也许贾斯汀不知道那种家伙，也许他知道。警方说他告诉娜奥米他抽大麻。贾斯汀说他只是想装酷来给娜奥米留下好印象。但可以肯定的是，他确实找到了一个愿意卖给他的人——他说那是他表弟认识的人。所以就在他们第一次聊到这个话题的几天后，贾斯汀带了一小袋大麻到学校给娜奥米。

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:27.910
<v Justin Laboy>And I was petrified. I was like, oh, my god. I'm actually going to do this. So we was in class. And I sat right next to her, and she was like, hey, do you have that? And I was like, you know what? Yeah, I do. And I was like, OK, we're going to wait in a few. We're going to wait a few. Because I'm not going to be like, oh, hey, give it to her in her hand, slide it to her.
<v Justin Laboy>我当时吓呆了。我就想，噢，我的天哪。我真的要这么做了。当时我们在上课。我坐在她旁边，她说，嘿，你带了吗？我就说，你知道吗？是的，我带了。我说，好的，我们要等一下。我们要等一下。因为我不会像，哦，嘿，直接交给她，递给她。

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:43.630
<v Justin Laboy>But she was like, OK. Put it in my purse. OK. She put her purse on top of my desk. And then I put-- slid it right in there She goes, OK. Now take the money. I was like, no, I don't need the money. It's OK. Just have it. You can take it. She goes, no, please. Take the money. You know, you make me feel bad.
<v Justin Laboy>但她说，好的。放在我的包里。好的。她把包放在我的课桌上。然后我把——直接滑进去了。她说，好的。现在拿钱。我说，不，我不需要钱。没关系。留着吧。你可以拿去。她说，不，求你了。拿钱。你知道，你让我感到难过。

00:35:43.630 --> 00:36:01.510
<v Justin Laboy>So 10 minutes passed, and then we're still arguing about it. I'm like, please. No, I don't want the money, you know? And she goes, no, just take the money. Take the money. Please, take the money. It would make me feel better. I'm just like, OK, you know what? Just so you could just shut up, I'll take the money, you know? Don't worry, you know? You're making me feel bad now that I'm taking money from you.
<v Justin Laboy>所以十分钟过去了，我们还在争论这件事。我说，拜托。不，我不想要钱，你知道吗？她说，不，拿钱吧。拿钱。拜托，拿钱。那会让我感觉好点。我就想，好的，你知道吗？为了让你闭嘴，我会收下钱的，你知道吗？别担心，你知道吗？你让我感到难过，因为我正在拿你的钱。

00:36:01.510 --> 00:36:02.710
<v Robbie Brown>How much money?
<v Robbie Brown>多少钱？

00:36:02.710 --> 00:36:04.180
<v Justin Laboy>$25.
<v Justin Laboy>25美元。

00:36:04.180 --> 00:36:05.930
<v Robbie Brown>And this was at school?
<v Robbie Brown>这是在学校里？

00:36:05.930 --> 00:36:07.910
<v Justin Laboy>Yes.
<v Justin Laboy>是的。

00:36:07.910 --> 00:36:26.100
<v Robbie Brown>Justin would later find out it's a felony in Florida to sell marijuana. And the penalty is even harsher for selling it on school property. In other words, Justin had made an irreversibly bad decision. The worst decision of his life, he says. And since he was over 18, he was legally an adult when he made it.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀后来才知道，在佛罗里达州贩卖大麻是重罪。在学校内贩卖惩罚更重。换句话说，贾斯汀做出了一个不可挽回的错误决定。他说这是他人生中最糟的决定。由于他已经18岁了，他在做这个决定时在法律上已经是一个成年人。

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:36.230
<v Robbie Brown>Finally, in May, the police did a big sweep. They arrested 31 people at several schools, almost all of them students who had sold small amounts of marijuana. Justin was one of them.
<v Robbie Brown>最后，在五月份，警方进行了一次大规模突击搜查。他们在几所学校逮捕了31人，几乎全部是出售少量大麻的学生。贾斯汀就是其中之一。

00:36:36.230 --> 00:37:02.650
<v Justin Laboy>And they said that I had sold to the undercover cop. And I'm-- I'm weirded out. I'm like, whoa, who did I ever sell to? And then it just popped into my head that the only person that I ever did give anything to was this female. And I was just-- I was shocked. I was-- I-- my emotions were running. And I was just like, oh, my god. This is so mean. This is all messed up. How can she do this, you know?
<v Justin Laboy>他们说我卖给了卧底警察。我——我感到很怪异。我就想，哇，我到底卖给过谁？然后脑海里突然蹦出，我唯一给过东西的人就是这个女学生。我当时——我很震惊。我——我——我的情绪在波动。我就想，噢，我的天哪。这太卑鄙了。这一切太混乱了。她怎么能这样做，你知道吗？

00:37:02.650 --> 00:37:23.420
<v Justin Laboy>I mean, if it was a guy doing this, if a guy ever tried to come up to me like this, I would have put him-- put my hand in his face, straight up and said, no. Get out of my face. Please, get out of my face. I don't associate with that, you know? But it's just the female-- it's a different feeling that you get for female than you would when you have your homeboy, or a guy come up to you and ask you a question like that. Oh, hey. Do you smoke? No, no, I don't. Get out of here.
<v Justin Laboy>我是说，如果是男生这样做，如果有什么家伙敢试图这样向我走来，我会让他——我会直接把手甩在他脸上，直截了当地说，不。从我面前消失。拜托，从我面前消失。我不搞那些东西，你知道吗？但这就是女性——你对女性的感觉和你面对你的哥们或男生走过来问你这种问题时的感觉是不同的。哦，嘿。你抽烟吗？不，不，我不抽。滚开。

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:36.470
<v Justin Laboy>It was a different feeling that when you get from a girl you like, you know, you're not going to turn down your-- the person that you want to be with.
<v Justin Laboy>那种感觉和你喜欢的女生走过来是不同的，你知道，你不会拒绝——你想要与之在一起的人。

00:37:36.470 --> 00:37:44.010
<v Robbie Brown>When I talked to the officer who plays Naomi, she said she had no regrets about her undercover work. She's a cop, and she sees it the way a cop does.
<v Robbie Brown>当我采访那位扮演娜奥米的警官时，她说她对自己的卧底工作没有任何遗憾。她是警察，她是以警察的方式看待问题的。

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:55.980
<v Naomi>These kids need to wake up. They need to realize they can't be doing this.
<v Naomi>这些孩子需要清醒过来。他们需要意识到他们不能做这种事。

00:37:55.980 --> 00:38:13.690
<v Robbie Brown>And using undercover officers has gotten results. The police in Palm Beach County tried the same tactics again at another group of schools last semester. So far, they've arrested more than 80 people. All have plead guilty to charges ranging from selling cocaine and pills to bringing weapons on school property.
<v Robbie Brown>而使用卧底警官已经取得了效果。棕榈滩县的警方上学期在另一组学校中再次尝试了同样的策略。到目前为止，他们已经逮捕了80多人。所有人都承认了从贩卖可卡因、药丸到在校内携带武器等各项指控。

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:35.710
<v Robbie Brown>Justin took a plea deal-- three years probation in exchange for pleading guilty to the felony charge. His lawyer told him that if he went to trial, it would be his word against Naomi's. And they disagreed about the drug deal. He says she kept asking him to sell her marijuana. She says he brought it up first. He says she insisted he take money for it. She says he just took the money without prompting.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀接受了控辩交易——承认重罪指控以换取三年的缓刑。他的律师告诉他，如果他上庭，那将是他的一面之词对峙娜奥米。他们对毒品交易有分歧。他说她一直要求他卖给她大麻。她说他先提出来的。他说她坚持让他收钱。她说他未经怂恿就直接拿了钱。

00:38:35.710 --> 00:38:53.430
<v Robbie Brown>Either way, the police had Justin's text messages telling Naomi he would get marijuana for her. So Justin's lawyer advised him, just take the plea. But before the deal came through, he spent more than a week in jail, going over and over in his mind what had happened, and still thinking in spite of himself about Naomi.
<v Robbie Brown>不管怎样，警方有贾斯汀发给娜奥米的短信，说他会帮她搞到大麻。所以贾斯汀的律师建议他直接认罪。但在达成交易之前，他在监狱里待了一周多，在脑海里一遍又一遍地复盘发生的事情，而且尽管如此，他还是在想娜奥米。

00:38:53.430 --> 00:39:07.060
<v Justin Laboy>I would have a good time when I would be with her. And I would be with her, like, all the time. And that's why when all this collapsed and caved in, I felt-- I felt so like-- it just hurt.
<v Justin Laboy>我和她在一起时会玩得很开心。我几乎一直和她在一起。这就是为什么当这一切崩塌、陷落时，我感到——我感到非常——它真的伤害了我。

00:39:07.060 --> 00:39:09.530
<v Robbie Brown>Have you had any contact with Naomi--
<v Robbie Brown>你和娜奥米有过任何联系吗——

00:39:09.530 --> 00:39:10.340
<v Justin Laboy>No, not--
<v Justin Laboy>没有，没有——

00:39:10.340 --> 00:39:11.000
<v Robbie Brown>--since you were arrested?
<v Robbie Brown>——在你被捕之后？

00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:11.300
<v Justin Laboy>Not at all.
<v Justin Laboy>完全没有。

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:12.710
<v Robbie Brown>Have you tried to send her any messages?
<v Robbie Brown>你试过给她发任何信息吗？

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:19.250
<v Justin Laboy>No. I don't have any contact with her whatsoever. If I would, I would love to have a conversation with her. You know? And no disrespect, or anything.
<v Justin Laboy>没有。我和她没有任何联系。如果我有，我很乐意和她谈谈。你知道吗？而且没有任何不敬的意思。

00:39:19.250 --> 00:39:20.300
<v Robbie Brown>What would you tell her?
<v Robbie Brown>你会告诉她什么？

00:39:20.300 --> 00:39:27.980
<v Justin Laboy>I would tell her, what the heck did she do? [LAUGHS]
<v Justin Laboy>我会告诉她，她到底干了什么？[笑声]

00:39:27.980 --> 00:39:58.230
<v Robbie Brown>Justin had planned to go into the Air Force after high school. He said he wanted to make something out of his life. Now with a felony conviction, the armed forces, any part of it, was off the table. Justin is applying to community college. He says now the whole thing seems kind of unbelievable. He was an honor roll student, a first time offender, with no criminal record. And of all the high schools, in all the towns in all the world, she walked into his.
<v Robbie Brown>贾斯汀曾计划高中毕业后参军。他说他想在人生中做出一番事业。现在有了重罪记录，武装部队的任何部门都对他关上了大门。贾斯汀正在申请社区大学。他说现在整件事看起来简直不可思议。他曾是一名优等生，初犯，没有任何犯罪记录。而在世界上所有的城镇里，所有的学校中，她偏偏走进了他的那一所。

00:39:58.230 --> 00:40:21.800
<v Ira Glass>Robbie Brown, these days he works at Bloomberg Media. Regular listeners to our program know the composer Lin-Manuel Miranda wrote a mini musical based on the story that you just heard. And it's really something. A video of it is at our website, ThisAmericanLife.org.
<v Ira Glass>罗比·布朗，他现在在彭博媒体工作。我们节目的常客都知道，作曲家林-曼努埃尔·米兰达（Lin-Manuel Miranda）根据你们刚刚听到的故事写了一部迷你音乐剧。那真的很棒。视频可以在我们的网站 ThisAmericanLife.org 上观看。

00:40:21.800 --> 00:40:35.560
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Cold Stone Dreamery. This next story, like that last one we just heard, is about a guy who yearns for someone-- yearns-- and this someone is not giving him much back. This story is from Ben Loory.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，冷石梦境。接下来的这个故事，就像我们刚才听到的上一个，讲述了一个渴望某人——渴望——而那个人并没有给他太多回馈的家伙。这个故事来自本·卢里（Ben Loory）。

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:50.170
<v Ben Loory>A duck fell in love with a rock. It was a large rock-- about the size of a duck, actually, that was situated off the bank of the river, a little past the old elm. Every day after lunch, the duck would saunter off to admire the rock for a while.
<v Ben Loory>一只鸭子爱上了一块石头。那是一块很大的石头——实际上大约有鸭子那么大，坐落在河岸边，就在老榆树后面。每天午饭后，鸭子就会溜出去欣赏那块石头一会儿。

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:56.260
<v Ben Loory>Where are you going? said the other ducks. Nowhere, said the duck. Just around.
<v Ben Loory>你要去哪？其他鸭子问。哪也不去，鸭子说。只是到处转转。

00:40:56.260 --> 00:41:07.490
<v Ben Loory>But the other ducks knew exactly where he was going and they all laughed at him behind his back. Stupid duck is in love with a rock, they snickered. Wonder what kind of ducklings they will have.
<v Ben Loory>但其他鸭子非常清楚他要去哪，他们在背后嘲笑他。笨鸭子爱上了一块石头，他们嗤之以鼻。想知道它们会生出什么样的鸭宝宝。

00:41:07.490 --> 00:41:24.610
<v Ben Loory>But there was one duck-- a girl duck-- who did not laugh. She had known the strange duck for a long time and had always found him to be a good and decent bird. She felt sorry for him. It was hard luck to fall in love with a rock. She wanted to help, but what could she do?
<v Ben Loory>但有一只鸭子——一只母鸭子——没有笑。她认识这只奇怪的鸭子很长时间了，一直觉得他是一只好鸟。她为他感到遗憾。爱上一块石头真是倒霉。她想帮忙，但她能做什么呢？

00:41:24.610 --> 00:41:47.270
<v Ben Loory>She trailed after the duck and watched him woo the rock from behind a tree. I love you, the duck was saying. I love you. I love you. I love you. I love you more than the stars in the sky. I love you more than the fish in the river. I love you more than-- more than-- and there he stopped, for he could think of nothing else that existed.
<v Ben Loory>她跟在鸭子后面，躲在一棵树后看着他追求石头。我爱你，鸭子在说。我爱你。我爱你。我爱你。我爱你超过天上的星星。我爱你超过河里的鱼。我爱你超过——超过——然后他停住了，因为他想不出还有什么别的东西存在。

00:41:47.270 --> 00:41:59.880
<v Ben Loory>Life itself? said the girl duck from behind the tree. She hadn't meant to pipe up. The words just sort of leaped out of her. The duck spun around to look at her. He was terrified.
<v Ben Loory>生命本身呢？躲在树后的母鸭子说。她并没有打算插嘴。这些话只是蹦了出来。鸭子转过身去看她。他吓坏了。

00:41:59.880 --> 00:42:08.330
<v Ben Loory>It's OK, said the girl duck, waddling out from behind the tree. I know you're in love with the rock. In fact, everyone knows.
<v Ben Loory>没关系，母鸭子说着，从树后摇摇摆摆地走出来。我知道你爱上了那块石头。事实上，大家都知道。

00:42:08.330 --> 00:42:28.780
<v Ben Loory>They do? said the duck. Yes, said the girl duck. Yes, they do. The duck sighed and sat down on the ground. If he had had hands, he would have buried his head in them. What am I going to do? he said. What am I going to do?
<v Ben Loory>他们知道？鸭子说。是的，母鸭子说。是的，他们知道。鸭子叹了口气，坐在地上。如果他有手，他会把头埋在手里。我该怎么办？他说。我该怎么办？

00:42:28.780 --> 00:42:40.200
<v Ben Loory>Do? the girl duck said. How can it go on like this? said the duck. I love a thing that cannot speak, cannot move, cannot-- I don't even know how it feels about me.
<v Ben Loory>做？母鸭子说。这种日子怎么过下去？鸭子说。我爱的东西不会说话，不会动，不能——我甚至不知道它对我有什么感觉。

00:42:40.200 --> 00:43:08.670
<v Ben Loory>The girl duck looked at the rock. She didn't know what to say. I know, said the duck, you think I'm crazy. You think it's just a rock. But it isn't just a rock. It's different. It's very different. He looked at the rock. But something has to happen, he said, and soon, because my heart will break if this goes on much longer.
<v Ben Loory>母鸭子看着那块石头。她不知道该说什么。我知道，鸭子说，你以为我疯了。你以为这只是一块石头。但它不仅仅是一块石头。它很特别。它非常特别。他看着那块石头。但必须得发生点什么，他说，而且要快，因为如果这样继续下去，我的心会碎的。

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:24.540
<v Ben Loory>That night, the girl duck had a hard time sleeping. She kept paddling around in circles, thinking about the rock, and the duck, and his heart that might break. She thought long and hard, and before morning, she had an idea. She went and woke up the strange duck.
<v Ben Loory>那天晚上，母鸭子很难入睡。她不停地转着圈游，思考着那块石头，那只鸭子，以及他那颗可能会破碎的心。她深思熟虑了很久，天亮之前，她有了一个主意。她去叫醒了那只奇怪的鸭子。

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:37.350
<v Ben Loory>Things happen when they must, she said, as if it were an extremely meaningful statement. So? said the duck. So I have a plan, said the girl duck. And I think that it will work.
<v Ben Loory>事情发生的时候必须发生，她说，好像这是一个极其有意义的陈述。所以？鸭子说。所以我有一个计划，母鸭子说。而且我觉得它会管用。

00:43:37.350 --> 00:43:56.950
<v Ben Loory>Well, what is it? said the duck, nearly bursting with excitement. We will need help, said the girl duck. And it will take some time. And also, we will need a cliff.
<v Ben Loory>嗯，是什么？鸭子说，几乎兴奋得要爆炸。我们需要帮助，母鸭子说。这会花些时间。而且，我们需要一座悬崖。

00:43:56.950 --> 00:44:12.680
<v Ben Loory>Two days later they set out. It took four ducks to carry the rock. They worked in teams and traded off every 15 minutes. Everyone joined in, even though they had laughed, for ducks are all brothers when it comes right down to it.
<v Ben Loory>两天后他们出发了。用了四只鸭子才搬动那块石头。他们分组工作，每15分钟轮换一次。每个人都加入了，尽管他们曾嘲笑过，因为当事情真的发生时，鸭子们都是兄弟。

00:44:12.680 --> 00:44:35.980
<v Ben Loory>The cliff is over that hill and then quite a ways to the south, said the most elderly duck. I remember flying over it when I was a fledgling. It looked like the edge of the world. The ducks trudged on under their rocky weight for hours. For hours, and then for days. At night they camped under hedges and strange trees, and ate beetles and frogs.
<v Ben Loory>悬崖在那座山后面，然后还要往南走很远，最年长的鸭子说。我记得我还是雏鸟时飞过它。它看起来像世界的尽头。鸭子们顶着沉重的石头重量走了几个小时。几个小时，然后几天。晚上他们宿营在树篱和陌生的树下，吃甲虫和青蛙。

00:44:35.980 --> 00:44:48.920
<v Ben Loory>Do you think it will be much farther? said one of the ducks. Maybe, said the old duck. My memory is not so good anymore.
<v Ben Loory>你觉得还要走很远吗？其中一只鸭子说。也许吧，老鸭子说。我的记忆力不如从前了。

00:44:48.920 --> 00:45:08.080
<v Ben Loory>On the sixth day, the ducks began to tire. I don't believe there is a cliff, said one of them. Me neither, said another. I think the old duck is crazy. My back hurts, said a third duck. I want to go home. Me too, said a fourth. In fact, I'm going to.
<v Ben Loory>第六天，鸭子们开始疲惫了。我不相信有悬崖，其中一只说。我也一样，另一只说。我觉得那只老鸭子疯了。我的背很疼，第三只鸭子说。我想回家。我也是，第四只说。事实上，我要回去了。

00:45:08.080 --> 00:45:29.000
<v Ben Loory>And then all the ducks began to turn for home. The rock fell to the forest floor and lay there. The strange duck looked imploringly at the girl duck. Don't worry, she said. I won't leave you. They watched all the other ducks flee homeward, and then they hoisted the rock onto their backs and trudged on.
<v Ben Loory>然后所有的鸭子都开始掉头回家。石头掉在森林地面上，躺在那里。奇怪的鸭子恳求地看着母鸭子。别担心，她说。我不会离开你。他们看着所有其他鸭子逃回家，然后他们把石头抬到背上继续前进。

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:39.600
<v Ben Loory>What do you think will happen when we throw it off the cliffs? said the duck. I don't know, said the girl duck. I just know it will be something.
<v Ben Loory>你认为当我们把它扔下悬崖时会发生什么？鸭子说。我不知道，母鸭子说。我只知道那会是特别的。

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:51.610
<v Ben Loory>Finally, they came to the edge of the cliff. The drop off was so great they couldn't see the ground. Just great, white clouds spread out before them like an endless, rolling cotton blanket.
<v Ben Loory>终于，他们来到了悬崖边。落差太大了，他们看不到地面。只有巨大的白云在他们面前铺开，就像一张无尽的、翻滚的棉毯。

00:45:51.610 --> 00:46:12.130
<v Ben Loory>It looks so soft, said the duck. Yes, it does, said the girl duck. Are you ready? The duck looked at the rock. This is it, my love, he said. The moment of truth. And whatever happens, please remember, always remember, I love you.
<v Ben Loory>它看起来好软，鸭子说。是的，确实，母鸭子说。你准备好了吗？鸭子看着那块石头。就是现在，我的爱人，他说。真相大白的时刻。无论发生什么，请记住，永远记住，我爱你。

00:46:12.130 --> 00:46:39.200
<v Ben Loory>And the two ducks hurled the rock off the cliff together. At first, the rock simply fell, like a rock one might say, like a stone. But then something began to happen. It began to slow. It began to grow. It began to change. It narrowed. It elongated. And it also spread sideways.
<v Ben Loory>两只鸭子一起把石头从悬崖上扔了下去。起初，石头只是简单地落下，就像人们所说的那样，像一块石头。但接着发生了变化。它开始变慢。它开始长大。它开始改变。它变窄了。它变长了。它还向两侧展开。

00:46:39.200 --> 00:47:01.730
<v Ben Loory>It's becoming a bird, the girl duck said. And it was. It was becoming a beautiful gray bird-- really not that unlike a duck. Its wings began to move slowly up and down, up and down, and it dove down, and then coasted up. It looked back over its shoulder at the two ducks on the cliff, and it called out just once-- goodbye.
<v Ben Loory>它正在变成一只鸟，母鸭子说。确实如此。它变成了一只美丽的灰色大鸟——其实和鸭子没什么两样。它的翅膀开始缓慢地上下移动，上下移动，它向下俯冲，然后顺势滑翔向上。它回头看了一眼悬崖上的两只鸭子，只叫了一声——再见。

00:47:01.730 --> 00:47:31.180
<v Ben Loory>And then it was going, going, getting smaller and smaller, flying off over the blanket across the sky. When they reached the pond, the other ducks gathered around and clamored to hear what had happened. The duck and the girl duck glanced at each other. Nothing, said the girl duck. It fell.
<v Ben Loory>然后它就去了，去了，变得越来越小，飞过天空中云层的毯子。当他们回到池塘时，其他鸭子聚拢过来，吵嚷着想知道发生了什么。鸭子和母鸭子对视了一眼。没什么，母鸭子说。它掉下去了。

00:47:31.180 --> 00:47:55.160
<v Ben Loory>In the days that followed, the duck stayed to himself. The girl duck went and swam around in circles. She thought about that rocky bird flying off into the sky. She saw it over and over in her mind. And then one day, not too many days later, she looked and saw the duck come swimming up. He was carrying a small salamander in his bill.
<v Ben Loory>随后的几天里，鸭子独自待着。母鸭子游着圈。她想着那只飞向天空的岩石鸟。她在脑海里一遍又一遍地看到那一幕。然后有一天，没过几天，她看见那只鸭子游了过来。他嘴里叼着一只小蝾螈。

00:47:55.160 --> 00:48:05.270
<v Ben Loory>For me? the girl duck said. And the duck smiled.
<v Ben Loory>给我的？母鸭子说。鸭子笑了。

00:48:05.270 --> 00:48:16.820
<v Ira Glass>Ben Loory, reading a story from his book, Stories for Nighttime and Some For the Day.
<v Ira Glass>本·卢里，阅读他书中的故事，《夜间故事及部分日间故事》（Stories for Nighttime and Some For the Day）。

00:48:16.820 --> 00:48:40.650
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, My Girlfriend's Boyfriend. Comedian Mike Birbiglia has this story of some of the extreme things that some of us put up with when we believe that we have found the one person in the world for us. He recorded this in front of a live audience and-- quick warning-- there's not really any sexual, prurient content in the story. But Mike does refer to the fact that sex exists and happens between people.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，我女朋友的男朋友。喜剧演员迈克·比比利亚讲述了这个故事，关于当我们相信自己找到了世界上那唯一一人时，我们所忍受的一些极端事情。他在现场观众面前录制了这个节目，并且——快速提醒一下——故事里并没有什么露骨的性内容。但迈克确实提到了性是存在的，并且发生在人与人之间。

00:48:40.650 --> 00:48:56.640
<v Mike Birbiglia>When I was a senior in high school, I had my first girlfriend, Amanda. And this was a big deal for me, because it was that-- that first time you fall in love, where you're like, oh, there is someone for me. This is it! Like, I found her!
<v Mike Birbiglia>我在高三时有了第一个女朋友，阿曼达。这对我很重要，因为那是——你第一次坠入爱河，你会想，哦，这世上有人属于我。就是她！就像，我找到了！

00:48:56.640 --> 00:49:19.110
<v Mike Birbiglia>And she was great. She was so beautiful, and she played tennis, and she wrote for the newspaper, and she was a-- she was a bad girl. And I was a kind of dorky nerd, kind of an outcast. This was at a boarding school that I didn't board at. I was only there because my family lived nearby. And she had major street cred. She had been expelled from her previous school for dealing acid.
<v Mike Birbiglia>她很棒。她很漂亮，打网球，为校报写作，而且她是一个——一个坏女孩。而我是一个书呆子，有点被排斥的人。那是在一所寄宿学校，但我没住校。我只是因为家人住得近才在那儿。她有很高的街头信誉。她因为卖迷幻药而被以前的学校开除了。

00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:22.590
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:49:22.590 --> 00:49:31.170
<v Mike Birbiglia>I remember at one point she said-- it was totally messed up because it was actually this other girl who was dealing acid, and I was framed. And I was like, awesome.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我记得有一次她说——那完全搞砸了，因为实际上是另一个女孩在卖迷幻药，而我被陷害了。我就想，太棒了。

00:49:31.170 --> 00:49:35.340
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:49:35.340 --> 00:49:45.840
<v Mike Birbiglia>I just-- I thought it was one of those things where we were opposites and we knew it, and that made it more exciting. Like, she wanted to be a writer and in student government, and I wanted to know what it was like to be cool.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我只是——我以为那是那种我们性格迥异而我们都知道的情况，这让它更令人兴奋。就像，她想成为一名作家和学生会干部，而我想知道成为酷女孩是什么感觉。

00:49:45.840 --> 00:49:47.740
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:49:47.740 --> 00:49:51.840
<v Mike Birbiglia>Well, I find that when you fall in love, you tend to overlook certain red flags.
<v Mike Birbiglia>嗯，我发现当你坠入爱河时，你倾向于忽视某些危险信号。

00:49:51.840 --> 00:49:59.190
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:01.050
<v Mike Birbiglia>One of them was that she was a liar.
<v Mike Birbiglia>其中之一就是她是个骗子。

00:50:01.050 --> 00:50:04.390
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:50:04.390 --> 00:50:21.210
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I don't mean that in an offensive way. At boarding school, lying is something of a way of life. I remember there was one guy in my class. He was a legendary liar. His name was Keith Robbins. And he used to lick his fingers like a bookie. And he'd go, yeah, yeah. Nice. Nice. Nice.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我不是以冒犯的方式那样说。在寄宿学校，撒谎是一种生活方式。我记得班上有个家伙。他是个传奇的骗子。他叫基思·罗宾斯（Keith Robbins）。他习惯像博彩经纪人那样舔手指。他会说，是啊，是啊。不错。不错。不错。

00:50:21.210 --> 00:50:21.950
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:50:21.950 --> 00:50:30.680
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he would lie about things that weren't important. Like, he'd be like, yeah. Yeah. Nice. My uncle's Tony Robbins, motivational speaker. Yeah. Nice.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他会为无关紧要的事情撒谎。比如，他会说，是啊。是啊。不错。我叔叔是托尼·罗宾斯（Tony Robbins），励志演说家。是啊。不错。

00:50:30.680 --> 00:50:33.000
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:39.510
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I found out later that that wasn't even true. But even if it were, it wouldn't be that impressive, you know, I--
<v Mike Birbiglia>后来我发现那甚至不是真的。但即使是真的，也没什么好印象深刻的，你知道，我——

00:50:39.510 --> 00:50:40.290
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:50:40.290 --> 00:50:51.420
<v Mike Birbiglia>So you didn't bother protesting it. You'd just go, oh, OK, Keith. You know? The other red flag was that Amanda used to say really mean stuff to me. And then she'd say, only kidding.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以你没费心去抗议。你只是会说，哦，好吧，基思。你知道吗？另一个危险信号是阿曼达经常对我说些刻薄的话。然后她会说，开个玩笑。

00:50:51.420 --> 00:50:53.460
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:50:53.460 --> 00:50:57.010
<v Mike Birbiglia>She'd be like, you're not good at anything. Only kidding.
<v Mike Birbiglia>她会说，你什么都不擅长。开个玩笑。

00:50:57.010 --> 00:50:59.040
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:50:59.040 --> 00:51:02.410
<v Mike Birbiglia>Nobody likes you at all. Only kidding.
<v Mike Birbiglia>根本没人喜欢你。开个玩笑。

00:51:02.410 --> 00:51:08.700
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:51:08.700 --> 00:51:12.410
<v Mike Birbiglia>The final red flag was that she told me not to tell anyone she was my girlfriend.
<v Mike Birbiglia>最后的危险信号是她告诉我不要告诉任何人她是我的女朋友。

00:51:12.410 --> 00:51:15.640
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:37.430
<v Mike Birbiglia>She had another boyfriend at home that she was in the process of breaking up with. And it was over, but if it got back to him, you know, it'd be bad. So she would go home every weekend and visit him, and at one point she said she had to go home more frequently, because his parents were sick. So she had to console him in that. And I thought, well, you know, the guy's parents are dying. So I ought to be understanding.
<v Mike Birbiglia>她在家里还有另一个男朋友，她正在和那个人分手。虽然已经结束了，但如果让那个人知道了，你知道，那会很糟。所以她每个周末都会回家看望他，有一次她说她必须更频繁地回家，因为他的父母病了。所以她必须以此安慰他。我心想，好吧，你知道，那家伙的父母快不行了。所以我应该体谅。

00:51:37.430 --> 00:51:39.820
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:51:39.820 --> 00:52:02.210
<v Mike Birbiglia>I also put up with it because I couldn't believe how lucky I was just to be with her. Like, in retrospect, I understand how selfish she was. But at the time, I didn't know that. When you're in a relationship with someone who's selfish, what keeps you in it is the fact that when they shine on you, it's like this souped up shine. And you feel like you're in the club. And you don't even know what the club is. You just you know you want to stay in it.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我也忍受了它，因为我简直不敢相信我有幸能和她在一起。回顾过去，我理解她有多自私。但在当时，我不知道。当你和一个自私的人谈恋爱时，让你留下来的是这样一个事实：当他们关照你时，那就像这种强力加持。你感觉自己进入了俱乐部。你甚至不知道那是什么俱乐部。你只是知道你想待在里面。

00:52:02.210 --> 00:52:16.230
<v Mike Birbiglia>We'd been going out two months. And we went on Christmas break and she invited me to meet her parents in New Hampshire. And this is very exciting. This is going to be my big moment. It would vindicate me and legitimize me as the main boyfriend.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我们交往了两个月。圣诞假到了，她邀请我去新罕布什尔州见她的父母。这非常令人兴奋。这是我的重要时刻。这会证明我并使我作为正牌男友的身份合法化。

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:29.250
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so I drive my mom's Volvo station wagon from Massachusetts to New Hampshire. And I meet her parents. And it's going really well. And then this guy shows up.
<v Mike Birbiglia>于是我开着我妈的沃尔沃旅行车从马萨诸塞州到了新罕布什尔州。我见到了她的父母。一切进展得很顺利。然后这个家伙出现了。

00:52:29.250 --> 00:52:31.440
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:42.920
<v Mike Birbiglia>And his name is Scott. And then the three of us are hanging out. And it dawns on me that I'm hanging out with my girlfriend's boyfriend.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他叫斯科特。然后我们三个在一起玩。我突然意识到我正在和女朋友的男朋友一起玩。

00:52:42.920 --> 00:52:46.200
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:52:46.200 --> 00:52:47.440
<v Mike Birbiglia>And it's going OK.
<v Mike Birbiglia>一切都还好。

00:52:47.440 --> 00:52:52.430
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:52:52.430 --> 00:53:02.740
<v Mike Birbiglia>He seemed like a good guy. He was an all-state wrestler. And he was remarkably nice. I could totally see what she saw in him.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他看起来是个好家伙。他是一名全州摔跤手。而且非常友善。我完全能明白她在看中他什么。

00:53:02.740 --> 00:53:04.590
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:53:04.590 --> 00:53:15.800
<v Mike Birbiglia>And there was some consolation because every time he would go to the bathroom, or go into the other room, she would be very affectionate towards me. She'd kiss my neck, or say something in her sweet voice. But then there was a moment where I was in the bathroom--
<v Mike Birbiglia>而且有一些安慰，因为每当他去洗手间或进入另一个房间时，她就会对我非常亲热。她会吻我的脖子，或者用她甜美的声音说点什么。但后来有一个时刻，我当时在洗手间里——

00:53:15.800 --> 00:53:18.040
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:53:18.040 --> 00:53:37.310
<v Mike Birbiglia>--and I thought, what's happening in the other room? The day took a strange turn when Scott suggests that we go hang out at his house. And so we go and I meet his parents. It's a very strange thing, meeting your girlfriend's boyfriend's parents for the first time.
<v Mike Birbiglia>——心想，另一个房间里发生了什么？这一天发生了奇怪的转变，当斯科特建议我们去他家玩时。所以我们去了，我见到了他的父母。这是非常奇怪的事情，第一次见到女朋友男朋友的父母。

00:53:37.310 --> 00:53:41.820
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:53:41.820 --> 00:53:47.450
<v Mike Birbiglia>Part of you is angry, for obvious reasons. And then part of you still wants to make a good impression.
<v Mike Birbiglia>一部分的你很生气，原因显而易见。然后一部分的你仍然想给人留下好印象。

00:53:47.450 --> 00:53:54.480
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:53:54.480 --> 00:53:56.940
<v Mike Birbiglia>As a side note, they seemed in perfect health.
<v Mike Birbiglia>顺便说一句，他们看起来身体健康得很。

00:53:56.940 --> 00:54:01.430
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:54:01.430 --> 00:54:08.980
<v Mike Birbiglia>I drive home, defeated. And I sort of knew that, at this point, this was her life. And I was like her secret life, like on Maury Povich.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我败下阵来，开着车回家。我大概知道，在这一点上，这就是她的生活。我就像她的秘密生活，像《莫里·波维奇》（Maury Povich）节目里那样。

00:54:08.980 --> 00:54:12.920
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:54:12.920 --> 00:54:32.090
<v Mike Birbiglia>So I was like, this is it. You know, I'm going to stick up for myself. It's either him or me. And I convinced myself that, given that choice, she would go with me because what we had was so special. So when we got back to school, I called her and I said, we need to talk. Let's meet at the hockey game. And she says, great.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以我心想，就是这样了。你知道，我要为自己挺身而出。要么是他，要么是我。我告诉自己，做出那个选择后，她会选择我，因为我们拥有的是如此特别。所以当我们回到学校后，我给她打电话说，我们需要谈谈。我们在冰球比赛见面吧。她说，好。

00:54:32.090 --> 00:54:50.870
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so I go to the hockey game and she's not there. Hockey game ends, still no sign. I had that pit in my stomach, you know, like this was going to be my moment. And I was going to tell her that she had to pick me or that's it. And so I start walking around the school, to the library, or the cafeteria-- the places she might be.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以我去了冰球比赛，她没在那。比赛结束了，还是没见人影。我胃里感觉很糟糕，你知道吗，就像这将是我的时刻。我要告诉她她必须选择我，否则就结束了。于是我开始在学校周围走，去图书馆，或者是食堂——她可能在的地方。

00:54:50.870 --> 00:55:03.790
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I asked people where she is. And finally, someone says, I saw her with Keith Robbins down at the tennis courts. I remembered earlier that day at lunch, Keith had said to me, I'm sleeping with your girlfriend. You know that, right?
<v Mike Birbiglia>我问别人她在哪里。最后，有人说，我看到她和基思·罗宾斯在网球场那儿。我记得那天午饭时，基思曾对我说，我正在和你女朋友睡觉。你知道吧？

00:55:03.790 --> 00:55:13.600
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:55:13.600 --> 00:55:45.290
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I thought, well, first of all, I hadn't even slept with my girlfriend. So that would be insane. And second of all, he's a liar, so he must be lying. I remember I said to him, yeah. I know. But at this moment it dawns on me that Keith was her new second boyfriend, and I was done. And it was that horrible, lonely feeling where you're walking around some place, and there are people all around, and there's only one person you want to be with, no matter how mean they've been to you.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我心想，首先，我甚至都没和我女朋友睡过。那太疯狂了。其次，他是个骗子，所以他肯定在撒谎。我记得我对他说，是啊。我知道。但就在这一刻，我突然意识到基思是她的新二号男友，而我完了。那是一种可怕的、孤独的感觉，你走在某个地方，周围全是人，但无论她对你有多刻薄，你只想和那一个人在一起。

00:55:45.290 --> 00:56:05.060
<v Mike Birbiglia>I just wanted to hear that-- only kidding. I remember people were coming up to me, and I couldn't even hear them. I couldn't even tell them what had happened, because even though I was being dropped, the relationship itself was based on a secret. And that spring, I graduated. Keith was expelled for making fake IDs in his dorm room.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我只想听到那个——开个玩笑。我记得人们走过来找我，我甚至听不到他们在说什么。我甚至无法告诉他们发生了什么，因为尽管我被甩了，但这段关系本身就是基于秘密的。那年春天，我毕业了。基思因为在宿舍做假身份证而被开除了。

00:56:05.060 --> 00:56:06.410
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:56:06.410 --> 00:56:11.810
<v Mike Birbiglia>He had built a life-sized license from Arkansas that people stuck their face in.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他做了一张真人大小的阿肯色州驾照，人们把脸伸进去。

00:56:11.810 --> 00:56:19.640
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:56:19.640 --> 00:56:25.870
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he would photo it, and then laminate it. He later took a job at Goldman Sachs.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他给它拍照，然后塑封。后来他在高盛工作。

00:56:25.870 --> 00:56:30.180
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:56:30.180 --> 00:56:32.660
<v Mike Birbiglia>That detail seems made up. It's actually true.
<v Mike Birbiglia>那个细节看起来像是编出来的。实际上是真的。

00:56:32.660 --> 00:56:37.000
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声]

00:56:37.000 --> 00:56:59.440
<v Mike Birbiglia>And Amanda was expelled the next year for dealing Ritalin. At boarding school, you can't go to the graduation if you're expelled. It's one of the shames of being expelled, and it's very strict. And I found out later that Amanda actually did show up to the graduation, in a disguise. She wore a wig and sunglasses.
<v Mike Birbiglia>阿曼达第二年因为卖利他林被开除了。在寄宿学校，如果你被开除，你就不能参加毕业典礼。这是被开除的耻辱之一，而且非常严格。我后来发现阿曼达竟然乔装打扮出现在毕业典礼上。她戴了假发和墨镜。

00:56:59.440 --> 00:57:15.330
<v Mike Birbiglia>My friends laughed about this, the way the friends do, to make you feel better when you've had your heart broken. But I could relate to her doing that because sometimes when you want to be in a place so badly, you'll do anything.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我的朋友们嘲笑这件事，就像朋友们做的那样，想在你心碎时让你感觉好点。但我能理解她那样做，因为有时当你如此渴望待在某个地方，你会不惜一切代价。

00:57:15.330 --> 00:57:17.790
<v Mike Birbiglia>[APPLAUSE]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[掌声]

00:57:17.790 --> 00:57:26.180
<v Mike Birbiglia>Mike Birbiglia, his one-man show, The New One, just finished its run on Broadway. He has a podcast called The Old Ones. It's available whenever you get your podcasts.
<v Mike Birbiglia>迈克·比比利亚，他的个人秀《新的一面》（The New One）刚刚结束在百老汇的演出。他有一个播客叫《老人们》（The Old Ones）。你可以在任何获取播客的地方听到它。

00:57:26.180 --> 00:57:49.610
<v Mike Birbiglia>[MUSIC - "WHOLE WILD WORLD" BY WRECKLESS ERIC]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[音乐 - WRECKLESS ERIC的《WHOLE WILD WORLD》]

